  "preamble": "Unrounding and lowering of the long close-mid front rounded vowel [øː] to [ɛː]. In Old Icelandic, ǿ (written œ), the i-umlaut of ó, merged with æ /ɛː/ around the 13th century; the vowel had probably already lowered to /œː/. Twelfth-century manuscripts of the Gray Goose Laws still keep the two apart, while later manuscripts confuse the letters (grœnn → grænn), and Old Norse dictionaries list Iceland-only spellings with æ. Icelandic æ later became the diphthong [ai].",
